@page "/generator"

<PageTitle>Generator reference - Linq2GraphQL</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">Generator reference</h1>
<p class="lead text-secondary">
    <code>Linq2GraphQL.Generator</code> is a dotnet tool, run as <code>Linq2GraphQL</code>, that reads a schema
    and writes a client for it. Install it as shown in <a href="getting-started#install-generator">getting started</a>,
    or use the <a href="generate-client">online generator</a>, which takes the same settings.
</p>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Usage"/>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>&lt;endpoint&gt;</code></td><td></td><td>URL of the GraphQL service to introspect. Not needed when <code>--schema</code> or the configuration file supplies the schema.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-sc</code>, <code>--schema</code></td><td></td><td>An SDL schema file to generate from instead of an endpoint, relative to the current directory. An endpoint and a schema file are mutually exclusive.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-o</code>, <code>--output</code></td><td><code>Linq2GraphQL_Generated</code></td><td>Output folder, relative to the current directory.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-n</code>, <code>--namespace</code></td><td><code>YourNamespace</code></td><td>Namespace of the generated classes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-c</code>, <code>--client</code></td><td><code>GraphQLClient</code></td><td>Name of the generated client class.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-t</code>, <code>--token</code></td><td></td><td>Bearer token sent with the introspection query.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-s</code>, <code>--subscriptions</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Include subscriptions. The client then needs <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client.Subscriptions</code>. Without it the generator warns if the schema has subscriptions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-nu</code>, <code>--nullable</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Generate a client annotated for nullable reference types.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-es</code>, <code>--enum-strategy</code></td><td><code>FailIfMissing</code></td><td><code>AddUnknownOption</code> gives every enum an extra <code>Unknown</code> member, so values added to the schema later don't break deserialization.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-d</code>, <code>--deprecated</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Include deprecated fields and enum values, marked <code>[Obsolete]</code>. Without it they are left out.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-ag</code>, <code>--agent</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Agent mode, in preview: also emit the schema as SDL so <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client.AI</code> can expose it to AI agents.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-sa</code>, <code>--standalone</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Write a copy of the runtime into the output, so the client needs no <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client</code> package. See <a href="generator#standalone">standalone clients</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-nowrap"><code>-cf</code>, <code>--config</code></td><td><code>linq2graphql.json</code></td><td>A json settings file, see <a href="generator#configuration-file">below</a>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>For example, this generates <em>SpaceXClient</em> in the namespace <em>SpaceX</em> into the folder <em>Generated</em>:</p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Example"/>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="schema-file">Generating from a schema file</h2>
    <p>
        Instead of an endpoint you can point the generator at a schema written in SDL, for example one exported
        from HotChocolate (<code>dotnet run -- schema export</code>) or taken from a schema registry. No server
        needs to be running, and the result is the same client the endpoint would give:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@FromSchema"/>
    <p>
        This is also a good fit for CI: check the schema file in next to the generated client, and a schema
        change shows up as a diff in both.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="configuration-file">Configuration file</h2>
    <p>
        Every option can also be set in a json file, which keeps a long command line out of your build scripts
        and gives you somewhere to check the settings in:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@WithConfig"/>
    <p>
        A file called <code>linq2graphql.json</code> in the current directory is picked up on its own, so in a
        repository that has one, regenerating is just:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Bare"/>
    <p>
        The generator prints the path of the file it read. An explicit <code>--config</code> always wins over the
        discovered file, and pointing <code>--config</code> at a file that does not exist is an error; only the
        default file is allowed to be absent.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="linq2graphql.json" Language="json" Code="@ConfigJson"/>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Command line</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>endpoint</code></td><td>string</td><td>required</td><td><code>&lt;endpoint&gt;</code> argument</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>schema</code></td><td>string</td><td>none</td><td><code>-sc</code>, <code>--schema</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>output</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>Linq2GraphQL_Generated</code></td><td><code>-o</code>, <code>--output</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>namespace</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>YourNamespace</code></td><td><code>-n</code>, <code>--namespace</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>client</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>GraphQLClient</code></td><td><code>-c</code>, <code>--client</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>token</code></td><td>string</td><td>none</td><td><code>-t</code>, <code>--token</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>subscriptions</code></td><td>bool</td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>-s</code>, <code>--subscriptions</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>enumStrategy</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>FailIfMissing</code></td><td><code>-es</code>, <code>--enum-strategy</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>nullable</code></td><td>bool</td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>-nu</code>, <code>--nullable</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>deprecated</code></td><td>bool</td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>-d</code>, <code>--deprecated</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>agent</code></td><td>bool</td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>-ag</code>, <code>--agent</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>standalone</code></td><td>bool</td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>-sa</code>, <code>--standalone</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>scalarMappings</code></td><td>object</td><td>none</td><td>no equivalent</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>descriptionAttributes</code></td><td>bool</td><td><code>true</code></td><td>no equivalent</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <code>endpoint</code> is required only in the sense that it has to come from somewhere, the file or the
            command line, unless <code>schema</code> names an SDL file to generate from instead.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>enumStrategy</code> takes <code>AddUnknownOption</code>; any other value means <code>FailIfMissing</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>token</code> is settable here for completeness, but a bearer token is better passed as
            <code>-t</code> than checked into a file.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>descriptionAttributes</code> controls whether schema descriptions are also emitted as
            <code>[Description]</code> attributes next to the XML doc comments, so tools that read them at runtime
            (JSON schema exporters, OpenAPI, AI function factories) see the schema's text.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Setting names are matched case insensitively. A name that is not in the table fails the run, so a typo
        like <code>scalarMapping</code> is reported rather than silently ignored. Everything in the file is
        optional, and an option passed on the command line always wins, so you can keep the shared settings in
        the file and override one for a single run:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Override"/>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="mapping-scalars">Mapping scalars to your own types</h2>
    <p>
        By default the generator maps the well known GraphQL scalars to .NET types (<code>Int</code> to
        <code>int</code>, <code>DateTime</code> to <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, and so on) and generates a
        <code>CustomScalar</code> class for every scalar it does not recognise. <code>scalarMappings</code>
        overrides both halves of that:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Map a scalar to a <strong>simple type</strong> and it is emitted as that type, with no
            <code>CustomScalar</code> class. <code>"BigInt": "long"</code> gives you
            <code>public long? Id { get; set; }</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            Change an existing mapping the same way. <code>"DateTime": "System.DateTime"</code> emits
            <code>DateTime</code> instead of the default <code>DateTimeOffset</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            Map a scalar to <strong>null</strong> to opt it out of the built-in mapping, so a
            <code>CustomScalar</code> class is generated for it and you control the conversion yourself.
            GraphQL's own <code>Int</code>, <code>Float</code>, <code>String</code> and <code>Boolean</code> can be
            mapped to another type but not opted out.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Scalar names are matched case insensitively. The target must be one of the supported simple types:
        <code>bool</code>, <code>byte</code>, <code>sbyte</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>short</code>,
        <code>ushort</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>uint</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>ulong</code>,
        <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>decimal</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>Guid</code>,
        <code>Uri</code>, <code>DateTime</code>, <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, <code>DateOnly</code>,
        <code>TimeOnly</code> or <code>TimeSpan</code>. Write it as the C# keyword (<code>long</code>), the type
        name (<code>Int64</code>) or the full name (<code>System.Int64</code>). Anything else fails the run,
        rather than generating a client that does not compile.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="standalone">Standalone clients</h2>
    <p>
        A generated client normally references the <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client</code> package, and an app gets one
        version of that package however many clients it uses. That is a problem when clients are built and
        shipped as packages of their own, by different teams: an app that references two clients made with
        different generator versions ends up with whichever runtime NuGet picks, which may not be the one either
        client was generated against.
    </p>
    <p>
        With <code>--standalone</code> the generator writes a copy of the runtime next to the client instead,
        under the namespace <code>&lt;namespace&gt;.Runtime</code>. The client then depends on nothing from
        Linq2GraphQL, so any number of standalone clients, of any generator version, can live in one app, next to
        clients that use the package.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Standalone"/>
    <p>
        The runtime lands in a <code>Runtime</code> folder in the output. <code>Runtime/Subscriptions</code> is
        only written with <code>--subscriptions</code>, and <code>Runtime/AI</code>, a copy of the agent tools
        from <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client.AI</code>, only with <code>--agent</code>. <code>Runtime/RuntimeInfo.cs</code> records the runtime
        version the copy was taken from and the packages it needs, and the generator prints them as
        <code>PackageReference</code> lines to add to the client's project, in place of
        <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="xml" Code="@StandalonePackages"/>
    <p>
        Code that uses the client does not change: <code>AddSpaceXClient()</code>, <code>Select</code> and
        <code>ExecuteAsync</code> work as before. Runtime types you name yourself, such as
        <code>GraphClientOptions</code> or <code>GraphQueryExecutionException</code>, come from
        <code>SpaceX.Runtime</code> rather than <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client</code>. In agent mode,
        <code>AsAgentTools</code> comes from <code>SpaceX.Runtime.AI</code>, so don't add the
        <code>Linq2GraphQL.Client.AI</code> package either.
    </p>
    <p>Things to know before choosing it:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            A runtime fix reaches a standalone client only when the client is regenerated with a newer generator.
            That is the point (each client keeps the runtime it was tested with), but nothing updates it for you.
        </li>
        <li>
            Don't edit the files in <code>Runtime</code>. The folder belongs to the generator and is deleted and
            rewritten on every run, and turning standalone off removes it.
        </li>
        <li>
            Each client has its own runtime types, so two standalone clients throw different
            <code>GraphQueryExecutionException</code> types; catch each one in terms of its own client.
        </li>
        <li>
            The packages the copy needs (<code>Microsoft.Extensions.*</code>, <code>Websocket.Client</code> and,
            in agent mode, <code>GraphQL-Parser</code> and <code>Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Abstractions</code>) are
            still ordinary NuGet references, so an app gets one version of each. They keep to semantic
            versioning, so the highest one requested works for every client.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private const string Usage = """
        Linq2GraphQL [<endpoint>] [options]
        """;

    private const string Example = """
        Linq2GraphQL https://spacex-production.up.railway.app/ -c=SpaceXClient -n=SpaceX -o=Generated
        """;

    private const string FromSchema = """
        Linq2GraphQL --schema schema.graphql -c=SpaceXClient -n=SpaceX -o=Generated
        """;

    private const string WithConfig = """
        Linq2GraphQL --config linq2graphql.json
        """;

    private const string Bare = """
        Linq2GraphQL
        """;

    private const string Override = """
        Linq2GraphQL --config linq2graphql.json -o=SomewhereElse
        """;

    private const string Standalone = """
        Linq2GraphQL https://spacex-production.up.railway.app/ -c=SpaceXClient -n=SpaceX -o=Generated --standalone
        """;

    private const string StandalonePackages = """
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory" Version="10.0.11" />
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http" Version="10.0.11" />
        """;

    private const string ConfigJson = """
        {
          "endpoint": "https://spacex-production.up.railway.app/",
          "client": "SpaceXClient",
          "namespace": "SpaceX",
          "output": "Generated",
          "nullable": false,
          "subscriptions": false,
          "deprecated": false,
          "enumStrategy": "FailIfMissing",
          "scalarMappings": {
            "DateTime": "System.DateTime",
            "BigInt": "long",
            "Json": null
          }
        }
        """;
}
